an": {"begin": 853, "end": 863}}, {"id": ["mesh:D005458"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "foam", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.6289510130882263, "span": {"begin": 853, "end": 857}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "foam", "obj": "cell_line", "prob": 0.576036274433136, "span": {"begin": 871, "end": 875}}, {"id": ["CL:0000891"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "foam cell", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.6642105579376221, "span": {"begin": 871, "end": 880}}, {"id": ["mim:108725", "mesh:D050197"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "atherosclerosis", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999812841415405, "span": {"begin": 942, "end": 957}}, {"id": ["mesh:D058226"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "fibrous plaque", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9450975060462952, "span": {"begin": 966, "end": 980}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "vascular smooth muscle cells", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.7833433151245117, "span": {"begin": 992, "end": 1020}}, {"id": ["mim:108725", "mesh:D050197"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "atherosclerotic", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999439716339111, "span": {"begin": 1100, "end": 1115}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "foam cells", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.8771790862083435, "span": {"begin": 1202, "end": 1212}}, {"id": ["mesh:D058226"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "atherosclerotic plaques", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999722838401794, "span": {"begin": 1238, "end": 1261}}, {"id": ["mesh:D013927"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "thrombosis", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.7326080799102783, "span": {"begin": 1291, "end": 1301}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "blood coagulation cells", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.9091459512710571, "span": {"begin": 1415, "end": 1438}}, {"id": ["mesh:D013927"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "thrombosis", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9998587369918823, "span": {"begin": 1463, "end": 1473}}], "text": "Atherosclerosis is defined as the thickening of the arterial wall towards the lumen. The thickening, called atherosclerotic plaques, is composed of macrophages, smooth muscle cells, lymphocytes, lipids (including cholesterol), and connective tissue. This thickening reduces the space in the blood vessels which is available for the blood flow (Widmaier, Raff, Strang, &amp; Vander, 2016).  &nbsp;  According to Gui et al, 2022 (Gui, Zheng, &amp; Cao, 2022), the pathology of atherosclerosis can be divided into three stages: (a) lipid-streak stage, (b) fibrous plaque stage, and (c) advanced lesions and thrombosis (Gui et al., 2022). In the lipid-streak stage, various forms of lipids undergo retention and trapping by arterial walls. Macrophages infiltrate into the intima of the arterial wall and uptake excessive lipids, leading to the formation of foam cells. Thus, foam cell formation is a key step in the initiation and progression of atherosclerosis. In the fibrous plaque stage, the vascular smooth muscle cells migrate to the intima of the arterial wall and generate the fibrous cap at the atherosclerotic site. The thick fibrous cap contributes to a stable plaque. Excessive accumulation of foam cells leads to necrosis within atherosclerotic plaques. In the advanced lesions and thrombosis stage, the necrotic core grows and the fibrous cap diminishes. The components of the necrotic core interact with blood coagulation cells and factors and trigger thrombosis.", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:39:53 +0000 2026"}